Facts you may use: the buffer keeps one slot empty so that `head == tail` always means empty and never full. Capacity is rounded up to a power of two so that the index wraps with a bitmask. `Ordering::Acquire` on the load pairs with the producer's `Release` store, so the consumer sees the slot's data before the new tail.

```rust
pub struct Ring<T> {
    slots: Box<[MaybeUninit<T>]>,
    mask: usize,
    head: AtomicUsize,
    tail: AtomicUsize,
}

impl<T> Ring<T> {
    pub fn with_capacity(capacity: usize) -> Self {
        let size = (capacity + 1).next_power_of_two();
        let slots = (0..size).map(|_| MaybeUninit::uninit()).collect();
        Self { slots, mask: size - 1, head: AtomicUsize::new(0), tail: AtomicUsize::new(0) }
    }

    pub fn pop(&self) -> Option<T> {
        let head = self.head.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
        if head == self.tail.load(Ordering::Acquire) {
            return None;
        }
        let value = unsafe { self.slots[head].assume_init_read() };
        self.head.store((head + 1) & self.mask, Ordering::Release);
        Some(value)
    }
}
```

